Title: The dependency guard cannot see skills/, and one undeclared import is already live
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---

2026-09-16T16:50Z claude created by the roar sweep after 0802: the same guard hole one directory over, with a live undeclared openai import in skills/external-peer-review
--- body ---
## Context

Found by the `/roar` sweep after `0802` merged. That ticket added `adapters/` to
`tests/repo_sources.py`'s `SCAN_DIRS` because the import-declaration guard could
not see a directory the same PR had just filled with Python. The sweep asked the
obvious next question -- which *other* directories hold Python the guard cannot
read -- and found one, with a live instance in it.

`SCAN_DIRS` is `("scripts", "tests", "hooks", "bin", "adapters")`. On
`origin/main` the repository carries eight Python files under `skills/`, none of
them scanned. `skills/external-peer-review/peer_review.py` imports `openai`,
which is not in `requirements-dev.txt`. So `tests/test_deps_declared.py` passes
while a shipped skill depends on an undeclared package.

The consequence is not abstract. `requirements-dev.txt` says in its own header
that CI installs from it and "a fresh machine reads it at setup". A fresh
machine therefore gets a harness whose `/external-peer-review` fails at import
time, and the guard built to prevent exactly that reports green.

## Evidence

The detector was validated before the finding was trusted: run against
`scripts/skill_frontmatter.py` it reports `yaml`, so it does see a third-party
import when one is present. An earlier control run against `adapters/perch.py`
returned only `skill_frontmatter`, a local module -- that control proved
nothing, and is recorded here because a weaker control almost passed for a
stronger one.

| Directory | Python files on `origin/main` | In `SCAN_DIRS` |
|---|---|---|
| `tests` | 78 | yes |
| `scripts` | 38 | yes |
| `skills` | 8 | **no** |
| `adapters` | 1 | yes (added by `0802`) |
| `projects` | 1 | no -- memory bodies, not code this repo runs |

Scanned directories currently carry no undeclared third-party import, so this is
the only live instance.

## Relevant files

- `tests/repo_sources.py` -- `SCAN_DIRS`
- `tests/test_deps_declared.py` -- the guard that reads it
- `requirements-dev.txt` -- where `openai` is missing
- `skills/external-peer-review/peer_review.py` -- the live instance
- `pytest.ini` -- `norecursedirs`, for whether `skills/` needs the same treatment
  `tests/fixtures` already gets via `EXCLUDED_ROOT`

## Actions

1. Add `skills` to `SCAN_DIRS`.
2. Declare `openai` in `requirements-dev.txt` with a version range, matching the
   file's existing pinning convention, and say in a comment which skill needs it.
3. Re-run the guard and fix whatever else `skills/` turns out to carry. Seven
   other files come into scope at once; this ticket owns their fallout.
4. Decide whether `projects/` should also be scanned or explicitly excluded.
   It holds one `.py` among memory bodies, so silence there is currently
   accidental rather than decided.

## Test

- A positive control first: add an undeclared third-party import to a file under
  `skills/`, confirm `test_deps_declared.py` goes red and names that file, then
  remove it and confirm green. The guard extension is worthless if it cannot be
  shown to fire on the directory it was extended to cover -- which is how
  `0802` verified the `adapters/` case.
- `make check` passes with `openai` declared.

## Verification

- [ ] `skills` is in `SCAN_DIRS` and the guard fires on a planted import there
- [ ] `openai` is declared, with the reason recorded next to it
- [ ] No other undeclared third-party import remains in any scanned directory
- [ ] `projects/` is either scanned or excluded on purpose, not by omission

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A fresh install reading `requirements-dev.txt` can run every shipped skill
- [ ] The guard's coverage is decided per directory rather than inherited
